        'Forward-looking information: how it works' => [
            'body' => '<p>IFRS 9 asks you to measure expected credit losses using not only what has happened, but what is likely to happen in the economy. The platform calls this forward-looking information, or FLI. This article explains the whole chain from the economic numbers you type in to the adjusted probability of default (PD) that the ECL run finally uses. Risk and finance staff who own the IFRS 9 model read this once; everyone else can go straight to the page they need. All of the pages sit in the sidebar under IFRS 9 Model Setup, in two groups: Forward-Looking Model and Management Overlays.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Forward-Looking Model</b> in the sidebar holds six pages, in the order you normally use them: Macro Elements, Scenario Profiles, Weighted Forecast, Credit Loss Data, Adjusted Forecast and Regression Analysis.</li><li><b>Management Overlays</b> holds three pages: Economic Scenarios, External Calculations and Calculation History. An overlay is a management decision applied on top of the model output; here it is the step that actually writes the adjusted PD onto the loan book.</li></ul><h4>The chain, step by step</h4><ol><li><b>Macro elements and their values.</b> A macro element is one economic series, such as inflation, the exchange rate or GDP growth. On Macro Elements you define each series once (name, code, unit, frequency, source) and then enter its values month by month. Each value is marked either Historical (what actually happened) or Forecast (what you expect), and a forecast value is tagged to a scenario inside a scenario profile.</li><li><b>Scenario profiles and their weights.</b> A scenario profile is a named set of economic outlooks, normally a base case, an upside and a downside. Each scenario carries a probability, which is its weight. The Status tick on the Scenario Profiles list turns green only when the weights inside the profile add up to exactly 100 percent.</li><li><b>Weighted forecast.</b> The Weighted Forecast page multiplies each forecast value by its scenario weight and adds the results together, giving one probability-weighted number per macro element per month. This is done by <code>MacroForecastWeightedService</code>: for every macro element it collects the forecast months tagged to the profile you chose, and for each month adds up value times probability divided by 100 across the scenarios. The answer is stored twice: as a new macro value with the source Weighted Calculation, and as a row on the Weighted Forecast list.</li><li><b>Credit loss data.</b> Regression needs a history of what you are trying to predict. Credit Loss Data holds observed monthly metrics per portfolio, such as PD, LGD, ECL, EAD or the non-performing loan (NPL) figure, entered by hand or imported from a CSV file.</li><li><b>Regression.</b> Regression is a statistical method that finds the straight-line relationship between one thing you want to predict (the dependent variable, for example NPL) and one or more things you can observe (the independent variables, your macro elements). Regression Analysis trains such a model with <code>RegressionService</code>: it lines up the months where the credit loss metric and every chosen macro element all have historical values, solves for an intercept and one coefficient per macro element, and reports R squared (how much of the movement the model explains), adjusted R squared, standard errors, t-statistics and p-values. You then Activate and Approve the model.</li><li><b>Adjusted forecast.</b> The Adjusted Forecast page is a calculator. You give it the intercept and coefficient from a regression, a baseline value and the macro forecasts per scenario with their weights; it shows the weighted macro path and the forecast value for each year, and the percentage change from the baseline.</li><li><b>Economic scenario set and external calculation.</b> On Economic Scenarios you record the scenario set (names and weights that must sum to 100 percent) that management has approved for the reporting period. On External Calculations you enter the reporting period, the base values, the regression slope and intercept and the weighted macro value for each future month. The page works out a predicted value per month and an FLI adjustment, which is the predicted value divided by the base predicted value, minus one. For example, a predicted value of 0.055 against a base of 0.050 gives an adjustment of plus 10 percent.</li><li><b>Post-FLI PD on the loan book.</b> When you click Update Loanbook, every loan in the reporting period is read. Stage 1 loans use the adjustment for the 12-month window; Stage 2 loans use the window closest to their remaining term; Stage 3 loans are set to a PD of 100 percent. Each loan\'s post-FLI PD is its pre-FLI PD multiplied by one plus the adjustment, floored at 0 percent and capped at 100 percent, and the adjustment itself is stored on the loan as <code>fli_adj</code>.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>On ECL Processing, the ECL Calculation form has a PD Type choice: PD Before FLI or PD After FLI. Choose PD After FLI to use the post-FLI PD written by step 8. The loan book screen and the forward-looking reports read the same column, so the figure you see on the Loan Book is the one used in the run. The ECL run never writes the post-FLI PD back; only the External Calculations page owns it.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>The Status column on Scenario Profiles shows a red cross.</b> The weights in the profile do not add to 100 percent. Open the profile with the eye icon and correct the probabilities.</li><li><b>Regression fails with "Insufficient overlapping data".</b> The credit loss metric and every chosen macro element must share the same historical months. Check both pages for gaps.</li><li><b>Update Loanbook says "No loans found for reporting period".</b> Import the loan book for that month first (Customer &amp; Loan Data, Imports), then run the update again.</li></ul>',
            'images' => ['fli' => 'The Forward-Looking Model group in the sidebar, opened on the Weighted Forecast page'],
            'routes' => [],
        ],

        'Macro elements' => [
            'body' => '<p>A macro element is one economic series that you believe moves your credit losses, for example inflation, the policy rate, the kwacha exchange rate or GDP growth. This page is where the model owner defines each series and keeps its monthly values, both the history and the forecasts for each scenario. Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Macro Elements.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Page title:</b> Macro Element Definitions.</li><li><b>Header button:</b> + Add Variable, which opens the Add Macro Variable window.</li><li><b>Table columns, in order:</b> Name, Code, Frequency, Data Source, Actions. Rows are sorted by name.</li><li><b>Action icons on each row:</b> a pencil (tooltip Edit Variable) opens the same window filled in; a task-list icon (tooltip Go to Values) opens the values page for that element; a red bin (tooltip Delete Variable) asks "Are you sure you want to delete this?" and then removes the element.</li><li><b>Add or Edit Macro Variable window:</b> fields Name, Code, Description, Unit, Frequency, Data Source, with Cancel and Save (or Update when editing).</li></ul><p>The values page, reached with the task-list icon, has its own layout:</p><ul><li><b>Title:</b> the element name followed by Values, with the unit in grey beside it, and a back arrow that returns to the list.</li><li><b>Header button:</b> + Add Value.</li><li><b>Filters:</b> Scenario (All Scenarios or one scenario), Type (All, Historical, Forecast) and Period (a date).</li><li><b>Table columns, in order:</b> Period (shown as month and year), Value, Scenario (shows Base when no scenario is set), Type (a badge reading Forecast or Historical), Source (a dash when empty), Actions (pencil to edit, red bin to delete). Ten values are shown per page with numbered page buttons underneath.</li><li><b>Add or Edit Value window:</b> Period, Value, Profile, Scenario, Is Forecast, Source, Notes, with Cancel and Save (or Update).</li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>Name</b> (required): the plain name, for example Inflation Rate.</li><li><b>Code</b> (required, must be unique): a short code with no spaces, for example CPI. The system rejects a code already in use.</li><li><b>Description:</b> optional free text explaining the series.</li><li><b>Unit:</b> how the value is expressed, for example percent or MWK per USD. It is shown beside the title on the values page.</li><li><b>Frequency:</b> Monthly, Quarterly or Yearly, or leave the dash for unspecified. This is a label only; values are always stored by month.</li><li><b>Data Source:</b> where the figures come from, for example Reserve Bank of Malawi.</li><li><b>Period</b> (values window, required): choose the month. The box accepts months up to the current month.</li><li><b>Value</b> (required): a number with up to four decimal places, for example 27.5 for 27.5 percent inflation.</li><li><b>Profile:</b> the scenario profile this value belongs to. Choose it first; the Scenario list is filled from it.</li><li><b>Scenario:</b> one of the scenarios inside the chosen profile. Leave it blank for a historical figure.</li><li><b>Is Forecast:</b> tick for an expected value, leave clear for an actual one. Only ticked values feed the Weighted Forecast; only clear values feed regression training.</li><li><b>Source</b> and <b>Notes:</b> optional; the placeholder suggests Central Bank or IMF.</li></ul><h4>To edit or delete an element</h4><ol><li>Click the pencil on the row, change the fields and click Update. The message Economic Option updated successfully confirms it.</li><li>Click the red bin to delete; confirm the question. Deleting an element removes it from every list that uses it, so only do this for a series that was set up in error.</li></ol><h4>To edit or delete a value</h4><ol><li>Open the values page with the task-list icon.</li><li>Click the pencil beside the value, change it and click Update; the message Value updated successfully! appears.</li><li>Click the red bin, confirm the question, and the row is removed with the message Value deleted.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>Forecast values tagged to a profile and scenario are what the Weighted Forecast page combines into a single weighted number per month. Historical values are what Regression Analysis lines up against your credit loss data to fit the model. The post-FLI PD on the loan book is only as good as these numbers, so record the source of every value.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>The statistic code has already been taken.</b> Another element uses that code. Choose a different one or edit the existing element instead.</li><li><b>The Scenario list is empty.</b> Choose a Profile first; scenarios belong to a profile.</li><li><b>You cannot pick a month after the current month.</b> The Period box on the values window is limited to the current month, so forecast values for future months cannot be typed here. Enter the forecast path on the Adjusted Forecast page or the External Calculations page instead, or ask your administrator to load future months.</li><li><b>The filters do not seem to change the list.</b> The filters are applied when you move between pages with the numbered buttons; on a list with a single page they have no visible effect.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Macro Elements.',
                'Click + Add Variable.',
                'Type the Name (for example Inflation Rate) and a unique Code (for example CPI).',
                'Fill in Description, Unit, Frequency and Data Source, then click Save. The message Economic Option created successfully appears.',
                'On the new row click the task-list icon (Go to Values).',
                'Click + Add Value, choose the Period, type the Value, pick the Profile and Scenario if it is a forecast, tick Is Forecast when appropriate, add the Source, and click Save.',
                'Repeat for every month until the history and the forecast months for each scenario are complete.',
            ],
            'images' => ['macro-elements' => 'Macro Element Definitions list with the + Add Variable button'],
            'routes' => ['macro-statistics.index', 'macro-statistics.store', 'macro-statistics.update', 'macro-statistics.destroy', 'macro-values.index', 'macro-values.store', 'macro-values.update', 'macro-values.destroy'],
        ],

        'Scenario profiles' => [
            'body' => '<p>A scenario profile is a named set of economic outlooks, normally a base case, an upside and a downside, each with a probability. The probability is the scenario weight: the share of the future you believe that outlook represents. The model owner creates profiles here, and every forecast macro value and every weighted forecast is tagged to one. Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Scenario Profiles.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Page title:</b> Scenario Profiles.</li><li><b>Header buttons:</b> + Create New Profile opens the Add Profile window; + Add Scenario opens the Add Scenario window.</li><li><b>Table columns, in order:</b> Scenario Code (the profile code), Scenario Name (the profile name), Created By (the number of the user who created it), Status, Actions.</li><li><b>Status values:</b> a green tick means the weights of the scenarios in the profile add up to 100 percent (within 0.01); a red cross means they do not, or the profile has no scenarios yet.</li><li><b>Action icons:</b> an eye opens the profile\'s scenarios page; a pencil and a red bin are shown but are not connected on this list, so editing and deleting are done from the scenarios page.</li><li><b>Add Profile window:</b> Profile Code, Name, Description, with Cancel and Save.</li><li><b>Add Scenario window:</b> Profile, Name, Description, Probability (%), Is Base Case, Tags (comma-separated), with Cancel and Save.</li></ul><p>The scenarios page, opened with the eye icon, shows:</p><ul><li><b>Title:</b> Scenarios, with Profile Code and Profile Name underneath and a Back button.</li><li><b>Table columns, in order:</b> Name, Probability (with a percent sign), Type (a badge reading Base Case or Alternative), Tags, Actions (pencil to edit, eye to open the same edit window, red bin to delete). Base case rows are listed first.</li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>Profile Code</b> (required, up to 255 characters): a short reference, for example FY2026-Q3.</li><li><b>Name</b> (required): a descriptive name, for example September 2026 outlook.</li><li><b>Description:</b> optional notes on the basis of the outlook.</li><li><b>Profile</b> (scenario window, required): the profile the scenario belongs to, listed as code and name.</li><li><b>Name</b> (required): for example Base Case, Upside or Downside.</li><li><b>Probability (%)</b> (required): a number from 0 to 100, for example 50. Make the weights of all scenarios in one profile add to 100; the system does not stop you saving a profile whose weights do not, but it marks it with a red cross and the weighted forecast will be scaled wrongly.</li><li><b>Is Base Case:</b> tick for the central outlook. Only one scenario in the whole system can be the base case; ticking it removes the flag from any other.</li><li><b>Tags (comma-separated):</b> optional labels, for example rbm, mpc.</li></ul><h4>To edit a scenario</h4><ol><li>Click the eye on the profile row to open its scenarios.</li><li>Click the pencil beside the scenario, change the fields and click Save. The message Scenario updated appears.</li></ol><h4>To delete a scenario</h4><ol><li>On the scenarios page click the red bin.</li><li>Read the warning: Deleting this will remove macro data related to this scenario. Are you sure you want to delete this scenario? Click OK only if you accept that every macro value tagged to that scenario will be deleted with it.</li></ol><h4>To activate a profile</h4><p>There is no separate activate button. A profile is ready for use when its Status shows a green tick. You choose which profile to use each time you run Weighted Forecast or generate regression predictions, so a profile that is complete is, in effect, active.</p><h4>What happens next</h4><p>Macro values entered on Macro Elements are tagged to a profile and a scenario. The Weighted Forecast page multiplies each scenario\'s forecast value by its probability divided by 100 and adds the results, so a base case at 50 percent, an upside at 20 percent and a downside at 30 percent produce one blended number per month. That number flows into regression predictions and, through the External Calculations page, into the post-FLI PD on the loan book and the ECL run.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Status shows a red cross.</b> Add up the Probability column on the scenarios page; adjust until it reaches exactly 100.</li><li><b>Profile created appears but the list does not change.</b> Refresh the page; the list is reloaded on the next visit.</li><li><b>The base case moved to another profile.</b> Only one base case exists across all profiles. Tick Is Base Case again on the scenario that should hold it.</li><li><b>The probability must be between 0 and 100.</b> Type a whole percentage, not a fraction such as 0.5.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Scenario Profiles.',
                'Click + Create New Profile, type the Profile Code and Name, add a Description and click Save.',
                'Click + Add Scenario, choose the new profile in the Profile list, type Base Case as the Name, enter its Probability (%) and tick Is Base Case, then click Save.',
                'Click + Add Scenario again for Upside and once more for Downside, giving each its Probability (%) so that the three add up to 100.',
                'Check that the profile row now shows a green tick in the Status column.',
                'Click the eye icon to review the scenarios; use the pencil to correct any weight.',
            ],
            'images' => ['scenario-profiles' => 'Scenario Profiles list with the + Create New Profile and + Add Scenario buttons'],
            'routes' => ['scenarios.profiles', 'scenarios.profiles.store', 'scenarios.profiles.update', 'scenarios.index', 'scenarios.store', 'scenarios.update', 'scenarios.destroy'],
        ],

        'Weighted forecast' => [
            'body' => '<p>The weighted forecast is the single, probability-weighted path for each macro element: every scenario\'s forecast value multiplied by that scenario\'s weight, added together month by month. This page runs that calculation for a chosen profile and lists the results. The model owner runs it after the forecast values are complete. Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Weighted Forecast.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Page title:</b> Macro Forecast Weighted.</li><li><b>Header button:</b> Calculate Forecast, which opens the Weighted Calculation window.</li><li><b>Filters:</b> Macro Variable (All Variables or one element), Start Period and End Period (month pickers). They narrow the rows shown on the current page.</li><li><b>Table columns, in order:</b> Period (month and year), Scenario (the profile name), Macro Variable, Weighted Value, Actions.</li><li><b>Action icons:</b> a calculator icon asks "Are you sure you want to rerun this forecast?" and recalculates; a red bin asks "Are you sure you want to delete this forecast?" and removes the row.</li><li><b>Pagination:</b> ten rows per page with page links underneath.</li><li><b>Weighted Calculation window:</b> Start Period, End Period, Scenario, with Cancel and Process.</li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>Start Period</b> (required): the first month of the forecast horizon, for example 2026-10.</li><li><b>End Period</b> (required): the last month, for example 2027-09. The pickers accept dates up to twenty years ahead.</li><li><b>Scenario</b> (required): the scenario profile, listed as code and name. Choose a profile whose Status is a green tick.</li></ul><h4>How the calculation works</h4><p>For every macro element, the system finds the months that hold forecast values tagged to the chosen profile. For each of those months it reads the value stored for each scenario in the profile, multiplies it by the scenario\'s probability divided by 100, and adds the results. For example, inflation forecasts of 20 (base, 50 percent), 15 (upside, 20 percent) and 30 (downside, 30 percent) give 10 + 3 + 9 = 22. Months where no scenario has a value are skipped. The result is saved as a forecast macro value against the profile with the source Weighted Calculation, and as a row on this list. In the current release every forecast month found for the profile is processed regardless of the Start Period and End Period you type, and running it again for the same profile replaces the earlier rows rather than duplicating them.</p><h4>To recalculate after changing a scenario value</h4><ol><li>Correct the value on Macro Elements.</li><li>Return to Weighted Forecast and click Calculate Forecast.</li><li>Choose the same Scenario profile and any Start Period and End Period, then click Process. The rows are refreshed.</li></ol><h4>To delete a weighted row</h4><ol><li>Click the red bin on the row.</li><li>Click OK on the question. The message Forecast deleted successfully appears. The weighted macro value itself stays on Macro Elements; delete it there if it must go too.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>Regression Analysis uses these weighted values when you generate predictions for a profile and period. The External Calculations page asks you to type the Weighted Macro Value for each future month; copy the figures from this list so that the FLI adjustment, and therefore the post-FLI PD on the loan book and the ECL run, rests on the approved weighting.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Process runs but no rows appear.</b> The profile has no values marked Is Forecast, or the values are not tagged to that profile. Check the Type and Scenario columns on the element\'s values page.</li><li><b>The weighted value looks too small or too large.</b> The scenario weights do not add up to 100 percent, so the sum is scaled. Fix the weights on Scenario Profiles and run Calculate Forecast again.</li><li><b>The calculator icon returns an error page.</b> The recalculate action is not connected in this release. Use Calculate Forecast for the same profile instead; it replaces the existing rows.</li><li><b>The scenario profile id field is required.</b> You clicked Process without choosing a Scenario. Pick a profile and try again.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Weighted Forecast.',
                'Click Calculate Forecast.',
                'Choose the Start Period and End Period for the horizon you are documenting.',
                'Choose the Scenario profile (it must show a green tick on Scenario Profiles).',
                'Click Process and wait for the message Weighted forecast calculated successfully.',
                'Use the Macro Variable filter to check each element\'s weighted values month by month.',
            ],
            'images' => ['fli' => 'Macro Forecast Weighted with the Calculate Forecast button and the Macro Variable, Start Period and End Period filters'],
            'routes' => ['macro-forecast-weighted.index', 'macro-forecast-weighted.store', 'macro-forecast-weighted.calculate', 'macro-forecast-weighted.rerun', 'macro-forecast-weighted.destroy'],
        ],

        'Credit loss data' => [
            'body' => '<p>Credit loss data is the observed history of the metric you want to explain with the economy: the monthly PD, LGD, ECL, EAD or non-performing loan (NPL) figure for each portfolio. Regression Analysis trains on it, and the External Calculations page uses one of its metrics as the PD proxy. Finance or risk staff maintain it by typing records or importing a CSV file. Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Credit Loss Data.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Page title:</b> Credit Loss Data.</li><li><b>Header buttons:</b> Add New Record opens the entry form; Import CSV opens the import page.</li><li><b>Summary tiles:</b> Total Records, Portfolios, Periods, Metrics (the number of metric definitions).</li><li><b>Filter bar:</b> a month picker, All Portfolios, All Metrics, with Apply and Reset.</li><li><b>One panel per portfolio</b> headed by the portfolio name and a count such as 0 total records. Columns, in order: Period, Metric (a badge with the metric name), Value, Source (Manual when blank), Actions (pencil to edit, red bin to delete, which asks "Are you sure you want to delete this record?"). PD and LGD values are shown as percentages; ECL, NPL and EAD with thousand separators. Five rows per portfolio per page. An empty panel reads No data available for this portfolio.</li><li><b>Add New Record page</b> (title Credit Loss Data / Add New Record, heading Add Credit Loss Data): Portfolio *, Reporting Period *, Metric Type *, Metric Value, Data Source, Notes, with Cancel and Create Record (Update Record when editing).</li><li><b>Import page</b> (title Credit Loss Data / Import): Portfolio *, Upload CSV File drop zone reading Select a CSV file, the note Supported formats: CSV, TXT. Maximum file size: 2MB, then once a file is chosen, Map CSV Headers to Credit Loss Metrics with a mapping table (CSV Header, Credit Loss Metric, Status), Data Preview (First 3 rows), Expected Import Result, and the buttons Cancel and Import Data (Importing... while it runs).</li><li><b>Create Definition page</b> (title Credit Loss Data / Create Definition): Metric Code *, Metric Name *, Description, a Common Credit Loss Definitions strip, with Cancel and Create Definition. Open it by adding /credit-loss-data/definition to the site address.</li><li><b>Period view</b> (title Credit Loss Data / month year, opened by adding /credit-loss-data/period/YYYY-MM to the site address): tiles Selected Period, Total Records, Portfolios, Total ECL, Total NPL; buttons Add Record, Previous, Next; filters All Portfolios and All Metrics; per portfolio a table with Metric, Value, Description, Source, Actions (Edit, Delete) and a summary of ECL, NPL, PD and LGD; an Edit Credit Loss Data window with Value, Source and Notes.</li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>Portfolio *:</b> the loan portfolio the figure belongs to.</li><li><b>Reporting Period *:</b> the month; it defaults to the current month. The record is stored against the first day of that month.</li><li><b>Metric Type *:</b> the definition, shown as name and code. The hint under Metric Value changes with it: PD and LGD are entered as a fraction from 0 to 1 (0.05 for 5 percent), ECL and EAD in currency, NPL as a percentage, Credit Rating as text.</li><li><b>Data Source:</b> Manual Entry, System Generated, External Data, Regulatory Report or Internal Model.</li><li><b>Notes:</b> optional.</li><li><b>Metric Code *</b> (definition): up to 50 characters and unique, for example NPL. Click a tile in Common Credit Loss Definitions (ECL, PD, LGD, EAD, NPL, STAGE, CREDIT_RATING) to fill the form.</li><li><b>Metric Name *:</b> up to 100 characters.</li></ul><h4>Import file format</h4><ul><li>A CSV or TXT file, under 2 MB, with a header row and at least one data row.</li><li>A column headed <code>period</code> holding the month as a date such as 2026-01-31, 31/01/2026 or 2026-01-01. Rows without a readable period are rejected.</li><li>One column per metric, headed with the metric code or a recognised name: <code>ecl</code> or ecl_value, <code>pd</code> or probability of default, <code>lgd</code>, <code>ead</code>, <code>npl</code>, <code>stage</code>, <code>credit_rating</code>. Header matching ignores case. Blank cells are skipped.</li><li>Optional columns <code>source</code> and <code>notes</code>.</li><li>PD and LGD must be between 0 and 1; STAGE must be 1, 2 or 3; other metrics must be numeric.</li><li>A row for a portfolio, metric and month that already exists is overwritten. Rejected rows are written to storage/app/public/failed_imports/credit_loss_data_exception.csv with the reason.</li></ul><h4>To edit or delete a record</h4><ol><li>Click the pencil on the row; the form opens with the record. Only Metric Value, Data Source and Notes can be changed. Click Update Record.</li><li>Click the red bin and confirm to delete. The message Credit loss data deleted. appears.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>Regression Analysis reads these records for the portfolio and metric you choose as the dependent variable, month by month, and pairs them with the historical macro values. The fitted intercept and coefficient then feed the Adjusted Forecast and External Calculations pages, and so the post-FLI PD on the loan book and the ECL run.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Please fill in all required fields.</b> Portfolio, Reporting Period and Metric Type are all needed.</li><li><b>Please select a valid CSV file.</b> or <b>File size must be less than 2MB.</b> Save the sheet as CSV and split large files.</li><li><b>CSV file must contain at least a header row and one data row.</b> The file is empty or has only headings.</li><li><b>Please map at least one metric column to continue.</b> No header was recognised. Rename the columns to the codes above and choose the file again.</li><li><b>Import failed:</b> followed by a reason. Fix the file and retry; nothing from the failed run is kept.</li><li><b>The record appears twice.</b> It cannot; a second save for the same portfolio, metric and month replaces the first.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Credit Loss Data.',
