        ],

        'Adjusted forecast' => [
            'body' => '<p>The Adjusted Forecast page is a self-contained calculator for the forward-looking adjustment. You give it a regression equation, a baseline value and a macro forecast for each scenario with its weight, and it shows the probability-weighted macro path and the forecast value for each of the next five years, with the percentage change from the baseline. Risk staff use it to test a regression result or to prepare figures for a committee before committing anything to the loan book. Nothing on this page changes stored data. Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Adjusted Forecast.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Page title:</b> Manual Forecast Input.</li><li><b>Forecast Calculation Parameters panel</b> with a Collapse / Expand toggle, containing: What are you forecasting? *, Regression Intercept *, Regression Coefficient *, Baseline Value (Current) * with the hint Current value for comparison (e.g., current NPL %), and a green box showing Regression Equation: Forecast = intercept + (coefficient x Macro Driver), which updates as you type.</li><li><b>Economic Scenarios section</b> with an Add Scenario button and the hint Enter macroeconomic forecasts for each scenario (e.g., Inflation Rate %). Each scenario card has Scenario Name, Probability (%), a Remove button (shown when there is more than one scenario) and a Macroeconomic Forecast (Years 1-10) grid of boxes Year 1 to Year 10.</li><li><b>Save this forecast session</b> tick box, which reveals a Session name box.</li><li><b>Generate Forecast</b> button, with a spinner while it runs.</li><li><b>Forecast Results panel</b> (after a run) with a Generated badge and its own Collapse / Expand toggle: Regression Equation Used, a Weighted Average Forecast table (Year, Weighted Macro Driver, Forecast Value, Change from Baseline) and an Individual Scenario Forecasts section with one table per scenario headed by its name and (Probability: n%), columns Year, Macro Value, Forecast Value.</li><li><b>Before any run:</b> No forecast generated yet, Fill out the form above and click "Generate Forecast" to see results.</li><li><b>Error box:</b> There were errors with your submission, listing each field.</li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>What are you forecasting? *:</b> Non-Performing Loans (NPL) Ratio, Expected Credit Loss (ECL), Probability of Default (PD), Loss Given Default (LGD) or Other. It labels the run; it does not change the arithmetic.</li><li><b>Regression Intercept *</b> and <b>Regression Coefficient *:</b> the intercept and the macro element\'s coefficient from a trained model on Regression Analysis, for example 0.02 and 0.30.</li><li><b>Baseline Value (Current) *:</b> today\'s value of the metric, for example 11.55 for an NPL ratio of 11.55 percent. Change from Baseline is measured against it.</li><li><b>Scenario Name</b> and <b>Probability (%):</b> for example Base Case at 40 and Upside at 25. Make the weights add to 100; the page does not check the total.</li><li><b>Year 1 to Year 10:</b> the macro element\'s forecast value for each year, for example inflation of 31.5, 28.5, 20, 16 and 16. The calculation uses Years 1 to 5; Years 6 to 10 are accepted but not used in the results.</li><li><b>Save this forecast session</b> and <b>Session name:</b> when ticked, a name is required. In this release the results are shown on the page and are not stored, so leave the box clear unless asked.</li></ul><h4>How the numbers are worked out</h4><p>For each year the weighted macro driver is the sum of every scenario\'s value for that year multiplied by its probability divided by 100. The forecast value is intercept + coefficient x weighted macro driver. Change from Baseline is (forecast value minus baseline) divided by baseline, shown as a percentage. The individual tables apply the same equation to each scenario\'s own values. The Year column of the weighted table shows calendar years starting from the current year.</p><h4>To add or remove a scenario</h4><ol><li>Click Add Scenario; a card named New Scenario with a probability of 10 appears.</li><li>Rename it, set its Probability (%) and fill in the Year boxes.</li><li>Click Remove on a card to drop it; the last remaining scenario cannot be removed.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>Nothing is written to the loan book from here. When the committee is satisfied with the path, take the intercept, coefficient and the weighted macro values to External Calculations under Management Overlays, where the FLI adjustment is saved per period and applied to the post-FLI PD that the ECL run uses.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Session name is required when saving the session.</b> Type a name or untick Save this forecast session.</li><li><b>scenarios.0.macro_forecast.0: The ... field is required.</b> A Year box is empty or contains text. Every used year needs a number.</li><li><b>The results panel disappears.</b> Results live only on the page; leaving it clears them. Generate again or note the figures.</li><li><b>Change from Baseline shows 0.00% everywhere.</b> Baseline Value is 0; enter the current value.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Adjusted Forecast.',
                'Choose what you are forecasting, then type the Regression Intercept and Regression Coefficient from your approved model.',
                'Type the Baseline Value (Current), for example the latest NPL ratio.',
                'For each scenario card type the Scenario Name, the Probability (%) and the macro forecast in Year 1 to Year 5; click Add Scenario for a Downside if it is missing.',
                'Check that the probabilities add up to 100.',
                'Click Generate Forecast.',
                'Read the Weighted Average Forecast table and the Change from Baseline column, then the Individual Scenario Forecasts.',
            ],
            'images' => ['adjusted-forecast' => 'Manual Forecast Input with the regression parameters and the first scenario card'],
            'routes' => ['forecasting.manual', 'forecasting.manual.process'],
        ],

        'Regression analysis' => [
            'body' => '<p>Regression is the statistical method that finds how much a credit loss metric moves when the economy moves. The page trains a linear model: a dependent variable (the credit loss metric you want to predict, from Credit Loss Data) explained by one or more independent variables (macro elements, from Macro Elements) over a training window of months. The result is an intercept plus one coefficient per macro element, with statistics that tell you how well the line fits. The model owner trains, reviews, activates and approves models here and can generate predictions from forecast macro data. Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Regression Analysis.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Page title:</b> Regression Models, with the line Manage and analyze regression models for credit loss forecasting.</li><li><b>Summary tiles:</b> Total Models, Active Models, Avg R², Approved Models.</li><li><b>Button:</b> Train New Model.</li><li><b>Table columns, in order:</b> Model (name and Created date), Portfolio &amp; Type (portfolio, then type and dependent variable), Statistics (R²:, Adj R²:, Periods:), Status, Actions.</li><li><b>Status badges:</b> Active or Inactive (whether the model may be used), and Approved or Pending Approval (whether a reviewer has signed it off).</li><li><b>Actions:</b> View opens the model page; Activate or Deactivate toggles the active flag after the question "Are you sure you want to activate this model?" (or deactivate).</li><li><b>Empty state:</b> No regression models, Get started by training your first model.</li><li><b>Train Regression Model page:</b> Model Configuration with Model Name *, Model Type *, Portfolio *, Dependent Variable *, Training Start Date *, Training End Date *; Macroeconomic Factors with Select All Variables and one tick box per active macro element showing its description, Source and Unit; a Validation Requirements notice; Cancel and Train Regression Model (Training Model... while it runs).</li><li><b>Model page</b> (title is the model name): tiles R², Adj R², Training Periods, Status; Model Configuration (Portfolio, Model Type, Dependent Variable, Training Period, Independent Variables); a Regression Coefficients table with Variable, Coefficient, Standard Error, t-statistic, p-value, the first row being Intercept; Model Status with Active Status, Approval Status (Approved or Pending) and Validation (Passed or Failed); buttons Activate Model or Deactivate Model and Approve Model; Quick Actions with Make Predictions and Export Model Data.</li><li><b>Generate Predictions page</b> (title Generate Predictions - model name): Scenario, Forecast Periods tick boxes with the note These periods have forecast macro data available, a Forecast Macro Data panel listing each element by code and name with its value per chosen period, the Generate Predictions button, and a Predictions History table with Scenario, Period, Predicted Value, Date (or No predictions generated yet.).</li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>Model Name *:</b> a descriptive name; the placeholder suggests Corporate PD Model Q4 2024.</li><li><b>Model Type *:</b> Probability of Default (PD), Loss Given Default (LGD), Expected Credit Loss (ECL) or Credit Migration. It labels the model.</li><li><b>Portfolio *:</b> the portfolio whose credit loss data is used.</li><li><b>Dependent Variable *:</b> the credit loss metric, shown as name and code, for example Non-Performing Loans (NPL).</li><li><b>Training Start Date *</b> and <b>Training End Date *:</b> months; the end must be after the start. Aim for at least 24 months.</li><li><b>Macroeconomic Factors:</b> tick the elements to include. The page recommends 3 to 8; with few months of data, fewer is safer.</li></ul><h4>Reading the statistics</h4><p>R² (R squared) is the share of the metric\'s movement the model explains; 0.85 means 85 percent. Adjusted R² lowers that for the number of variables used. The p-value of a coefficient is the chance the true effect is zero; under 0.05 is treated as significant. Validation shows Passed when R² is at least 0.7, adjusted R² at least 0.65 and the training periods at least 24. The notice on the training page also lists significant coefficients (p-value below 0.05) as a requirement to check.</p><h4>To activate and approve a model</h4><ol><li>Open the model with View.</li><li>Click Activate Model and confirm.</li><li>Click Approve Model and confirm "Are you sure you want to approve this model for production use?". The message Model approved. appears and the status badge changes to Approved.</li></ol><h4>To generate predictions</h4><ol><li>On the model page click Make Predictions.</li><li>Choose the Scenario profile and tick the Forecast Periods you need; the Forecast Macro Data panel fills with the profile\'s values for those months.</li><li>Click Generate Predictions. The message Predictions generated successfully! appears and the rows join Predictions History. Each prediction is intercept + coefficient x macro value for each element, floored at zero.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>To apply a model to the loan book, copy its Intercept and the coefficient of the macro element you will forecast into Regression Intercept and Regression Slope on External Calculations under Management Overlays, together with the weighted macro values per period. That page saves the FLI adjustment and writes the post-FLI PD used by the ECL run. Export Model Data is a placeholder in this release and shows Export functionality to be implemented.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Failed to train model: Insufficient overlapping data for regression analysis.</b> The message lists how many months each variable has and which months overlap. Fill the gaps on Credit Loss Data or Macro Elements, or narrow the training window.</li><li><b>Failed to train model: Matrix is singular and cannot be inverted.</b> Two chosen elements move identically, or there are fewer months than variables. Remove a variable.</li><li><b>The train end must be a date after train start.</b> Swap the dates.</li><li><b>No macro data available. Please select a scenario and periods first.</b> Choose a Scenario and tick at least one period before Generate Predictions.</li><li><b>No periods are listed under Forecast Periods.</b> None of the model\'s macro elements has values marked Is Forecast. Add them on Macro Elements.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Forward-Looking Model, and choose Regression Analysis.',
                'Click Train New Model.',
                'Type the Model Name and choose the Model Type, Portfolio and Dependent Variable.',
                'Set the Training Start Date and Training End Date to cover at least 24 months of credit loss data.',
                'Tick the macro elements to use under Macroeconomic Factors.',
                'Click Train Regression Model and wait for the model page to open with the message Regression model trained successfully!.',
                'Check R², Adj R² and the p-value column; Validation should read Passed.',
                'Click Activate Model, then Approve Model, confirming each question.',
            ],
            'images' => [
                'regression' => 'Regression Models list with the summary tiles and Train New Model button',
                'regression-create' => 'Train Regression Model form with the Validation Requirements notice',
            ],
            'routes' => ['regression.index', 'regression.create', 'regression.store', 'regression.view', 'regression.toggle-active', 'regression.approve', 'regression.predict', 'regression.predict.store', 'regression.forecast.data'],
        ],

        'Economic scenarios (management overlays)' => [
            'body' => '<p>An economic scenario set is the list of scenario names and weights that management has approved for a reporting period, for example Base Case 50 percent, Upside 20 percent and Downside 30 percent. It is the documented basis for the weighted macro values you enter on External Calculations, and it is stored with every calculation so that the audit trail shows which weighting was used. A management overlay is an adjustment applied on top of the model output by management decision; the scenario set is the first part of that decision. Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Management Overlays, and choose Economic Scenarios.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Page title:</b> Economic Scenario Sets.</li><li><b>Filter bar:</b> a Filter drop-down, a Search... box that matches the name or description as you type, and Reset.</li><li><b>Header button:</b> Create Scenario Set.</li><li><b>Table columns, in order:</b> ID, Name, Description, Status, Created By, Action. Twenty sets per page with Previous and Next links.</li><li><b>Status values:</b> Active (green) means the set is offered on External Calculations; Inactive means it is kept for record only.</li><li><b>Action icons:</b> a pencil (tooltip Edit) opens the edit page; a red bin (tooltip Delete) opens the Delete Scenario Set window, which reads Are you sure you want to delete this scenario set? This action cannot be undone. with Cancel and Delete.</li><li><b>Empty state:</b> No Scenario Sets found.</li><li><b>Create and Edit pages</b> (titles Economic Scenario Sets / Create and Economic Scenario Sets / Edit): Scenario Set Name, Description, Active Status tick box, a Scenarios section with + Add Scenario, one row per scenario with Scenario Name, Probability (%) and a bin icon, a running Total Probability: n% line, and the button Create Scenario Set or Update Scenario Set.</li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>Scenario Set Name</b> (required, unique): for example Inhouse View or September 2026 MPC view.</li><li><b>Description:</b> optional; say where the weights came from.</li><li><b>Active Status:</b> ticked by default. Only active sets appear in the Scenario Set list on External Calculations.</li><li><b>Scenario Name</b> (required per row): the placeholder suggests Base Case, Upside, Downside. New sets start with those three rows at 50, 20 and 30.</li><li><b>Probability (%)</b> (required per row): 0 to 100 with up to two decimals. At least two rows are needed and the Total Probability must be exactly 100%; the save button stays greyed out until it is.</li></ul><h4>To edit a set</h4><ol><li>Click the pencil, or the set\'s ID or Name.</li><li>Change the fields or rows, keeping Total Probability at 100%.</li><li>Click Update Scenario Set. The message Scenario set "name" updated successfully. appears. The rows are replaced in full, so the order you leave them in is the order stored.</li></ol><h4>To retire a set</h4><ol><li>Open it with the pencil, untick Active Status and click Update Scenario Set. It stays in the history but disappears from External Calculations.</li><li>Only delete a set that was never used in a calculation; Calculation History shows the set name on each record.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>On External Calculations you choose the set in the Scenario Set list. The set is saved with the calculation parameters and shown, with its weights, on Calculation History. The weighted macro value for each period is typed on that page rather than calculated from the set, so use the same weights when you blend the scenario paths on Weighted Forecast or Adjusted Forecast. The FLI adjustments saved for the reporting period and set are then applied to the post-FLI PD on the loan book, which the ECL run uses when PD After FLI is chosen.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Create Scenario Set is greyed out.</b> Total Probability is not exactly 100%. Adjust a row.</li><li><b>Scenario probabilities must sum to 100%. Current total: n%.</b> The server re-checks the total; correct it and save again.</li><li><b>The name has already been taken.</b> Set names are unique; add the period to the name.</li><li><b>The scenarios field must have at least 2 items.</b> Add a second row.</li><li><b>The set is missing on External Calculations.</b> It is Inactive; edit it and tick Active Status.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Management Overlays, and choose Economic Scenarios.',
                'Click Create Scenario Set.',
                'Type the Scenario Set Name and a Description, and leave Active Status ticked.',
                'Edit the three default rows (Base Case, Upside, Downside) or click + Add Scenario for more, giving each a Probability (%).',
                'Check that the line reads Total Probability: 100%.',
                'Click Create Scenario Set and confirm the message Scenario set "name" created successfully.',
            ],
            'images' => ['economic-scenarios' => 'Economic Scenario Sets list showing an Active set with the edit and delete icons'],
            'routes' => ['fli.scenarios.index', 'fli.scenarios.create', 'fli.scenarios.store', 'fli.scenarios.edit', 'fli.scenarios.update', 'fli.scenarios.destroy'],
        ],

        'External calculations and calculation history' => [
            'body' => '<p>An external calculation is a forward-looking adjustment whose inputs were worked out outside the platform or on its own pages (a regression slope and intercept, a base level, and a weighted macro value per future month) and are then recorded, turned into an FLI adjustment per period and applied to every loan in a reporting period. It is the management overlay that writes the post-FLI PD. Calculation History is the audit trail of every such run. The model owner performs the calculation; reviewers read the history. Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Management Overlays, and choose External Calculations or Calculation History.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Page title:</b> FLI External Calculations, laid out as three numbered steps that appear one after the other.</li><li><b>1. Configuration &amp; Parameters:</b> Scenario Set, Reporting Date (YYYY-MM), Number of Forecasting Periods, Forecasting Period Length (Months), Economic Data Statistic, PD Proxy Statistic, Base Forecast Period (YYYY-MM), Base Macro Data Value, Base PD Proxy Value; a green Regression Model Parameters box with Regression Slope (hint Coefficient for macro variable) and Regression Intercept (hint Constant term in regression); a Supporting Documentation box with Attach File (Optional) and the note Upload regression analysis, macro forecasts, or supporting documentation (Max 10MB); an error list headed Please fix the following errors:; and the button Save Parameters &amp; Generate Forecast Table (Processing... while it runs).</li><li><b>2. Forecast Generation:</b> a table with Period (0, 1, 2 ...), Date (the month), Weighted Macro Value (an editable box), Predicted Value and FLI Adjustment (a percentage), then the button Save Adjustments (Saving... while it runs).</li><li><b>3. Apply to Loanbook:</b> the note Adjustments have been saved. You can now apply these FLI adjustments to the loan book. This process will update the pd_post_fli_adj and fli_adj columns for all loans in the selected reporting period., the button Update Loanbook (Updating... while it runs) and, afterwards, an Update Complete! panel with Total Loans Processed, Loans Updated, Stage 1 Updated, Stage 2 Updated, Stage 3 Skipped, No Matching FLI, Floored at 0%, Capped at 100%.</li><li><b>Calculation History page:</b> title FLI Calculation History; a panel External Calculation Records with a New Calculation button; tiles Total Calculations, Reporting Periods, Total Adjustments; a Search box (Search by period, statistic, creator...) and Filter by Statistic; a table with Reporting Period (and n adjustments), Scenario Set (with each scenario and its weight), Economic Statistic (with Base: value), PD Proxy (with Base: n%), Forecasting (n periods, m months each, Base: period), Regression (Slope: and Intercept:), Created By, Created At, Actions (Details or Hide). Details expands the row to show Loan Book Impact (Total Loans Updated, Average FLI Adjustment, Min FLI Adjustment, Max FLI Adjustment, or No loan book updates found) and Parameter Summary (Calculation ID, Total Forecast Months, Regression Formula). Empty state: No calculations found, with Get started by creating a new calculation or Try adjusting your filters.</li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>Scenario Set:</b> an active set from Economic Scenarios.</li><li><b>Reporting Date (YYYY-MM):</b> the month-end being adjusted; every loan in this loan-book period is updated in step 3.</li><li><b>Number of Forecasting Periods</b> (1 to 120, default 12) and <b>Forecasting Period Length (Months)</b> (1 to 12, default 1): together they set the horizon. Keep 12 periods of 1 month so that a 12-month window exists for Stage 1 loans; extend the count to cover the longest remaining term of your Stage 2 loans.</li><li><b>Economic Data Statistic:</b> the macro element driving the adjustment. <b>PD Proxy Statistic:</b> the credit loss metric the regression predicts.</li><li><b>Base Forecast Period (YYYY-MM), Base Macro Data Value, Base PD Proxy Value</b> (0 to 100): the starting point; the base macro value becomes the Weighted Macro Value of period 0.</li><li><b>Regression Slope</b> and <b>Regression Intercept:</b> the coefficient and intercept from Regression Analysis or an external study. Predicted value for each period is slope x weighted macro value + intercept.</li><li><b>Attach File (Optional):</b> PDF, Excel, CSV or Word up to 10 MB, recorded as supporting evidence.</li><li><b>Weighted Macro Value</b> (step 2): type the probability-weighted macro figure for each month, taken from Weighted Forecast. Predicted Value and FLI Adjustment recalculate as you type; FLI Adjustment is predicted value divided by the period 0 predicted value, minus one.</li></ul><h4>How the loan book is updated</h4><p>Stage 3 loans (post-qualitative stage) are skipped and given a PD of 100 percent. Stage 1 loans take the adjustment saved for a 12-month window; Stage 2 loans take the window equal to their remaining term in months, or the nearest saved window. Post-FLI PD is pre-FLI PD x (1 + FLI adjustment), floored at 0 and capped at 100 percent; the counts of floored and capped loans are reported. The adjustment is stored on each loan as fli_adj.</p><h4>To review a past run</h4><ol><li>Open Calculation History.</li><li>Type part of the period, set name or user in Search.</li><li>Click Details on the row to see Loan Book Impact and the Regression Formula used.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>The post-FLI PD now sits on every loan in the period. On ECL Processing, choose PD After FLI as the PD Type when you run ECL Calculation. The Loan Book, the forward-looking reports and the dashboard read the same column. Running the calculation again for the same period and set replaces the saved adjustments, and Update Loanbook overwrites the PDs, so re-run the ECL afterwards.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>Please fix the following errors:</b> followed by field names. Every field in step 1 except the attachment is required; dates must be YYYY-MM.</li><li><b>Provide regression slope &amp; intercept, or select a trained regression model.</b> Both regression boxes are empty.</li><li><b>File size exceeds 10MB limit.</b> Compress or split the attachment.</li><li><b>Failed to update loanbook: No loans found for reporting period YYYY-MM.</b> Import the loan book for that month first.</li><li><b>No Matching FLI is high.</b> No adjustment was saved for the windows those loans need; increase Number of Forecasting Periods and repeat steps 1 to 3.</li><li><b>Loan Book Impact does not match the FLI Adjustment column you typed.</b> Report it through Support Tickets quoting the Calculation ID from Parameter Summary.</li><li><b>Filter by Statistic returns nothing.</b> Its fixed choices do not match statistics stored by reference; use Search instead.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the sidebar, expand IFRS 9 Model Setup, then Management Overlays, and choose External Calculations.',
                'Choose the Scenario Set and the Reporting Date (YYYY-MM).',
                'Set Number of Forecasting Periods and Forecasting Period Length (Months) so the horizon covers 12 months and the longest Stage 2 remaining term.',
                'Choose the Economic Data Statistic and PD Proxy Statistic, then enter Base Forecast Period (YYYY-MM), Base Macro Data Value and Base PD Proxy Value.',
                'Type the Regression Slope and Regression Intercept from your approved model and attach the supporting file if you have one.',
                'Click Save Parameters & Generate Forecast Table and wait for the messages Parameters saved successfully! and Forecast table generated successfully!.',
                'In the Forecast Generation table type the Weighted Macro Value for each period and check the FLI Adjustment column.',
                'Click Save Adjustments and wait for Adjustments saved successfully!.',
                'Click Update Loanbook, click OK on "This will update PD values for all loans in the reporting period. Continue?", and read the Update Complete! counts.',
                'Open Calculation History and click Details on the new row to confirm the Loan Book Impact.',
            ],
            'images' => [
                'external-calculations' => 'FLI External Calculations step 1: Configuration & Parameters with the Regression Model Parameters box',
                'calculation-history' => 'FLI Calculation History with the summary tiles, search and record table',
            ],
            'routes' => ['fli.external.index', 'fli.external.list', 'fli.external.save-parameters', 'fli.external.generate', 'fli.external.save', 'fli.external.update-loanbook'],
        ],
    ],
];
